\documentclass{article}
\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}

\title{Segment Performance and the Reorganisation\\ of a Diversified Manufacturer}
\author{Case study --- N.~Whitfield}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Background}
The firm entered the review period with three reporting
segments and a corporate centre whose costs were allocated
on a revenue basis.
The board asked for a like-for-like comparison of segment
performance before deciding on the proposed reorganisation.

\section{Segment Results}
Table~\ref{tab:segments} summarises revenue, growth, and
operating margin for the continuing segments.
\begin{table}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\hline
Segment & Revenue & Growth & Margin \\
\hline
Healthcare & 20.1 & 6.3 & 0.41 \\
Consumer & 15.8 & 2.9 & 0.33 \\
Industrial & 9.6 & 1.8 & 0.27 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Segment performance for the review year.
Revenue in billions, growth in percent, margin as a share.}
\label{tab:segments}
\end{table}
The healthcare segment remained the largest contributor,
while the industrial segment lagged on every measure.

\section{Discussion}
The research and development services unit, currently folded
into the corporate centre, is reported separately from this
year onward because its economics differ from the other
segments: revenue is contract-based and margins are thin
but stable.

\section{Conclusion}
The reorganisation makes the segment structure match the
way the businesses are actually run, and the new disclosure
should improve the comparability of future reports.
\end{document}

